The old path reports a resource limit in every larger case. Its zero-loss bound
is already exact. Under tolerance1 its loose[-1,1]bound already establishes
equivalence, so tightening that interval does not improve the decision.
Three cases gain a resolved preference; the competition remains unresolved.
The conversion adds no decision value over the competent conventional endpoint
method, which ties every bound.

In the competition case, the audit correctly says that the hypothetical premise
set is insufficient to certify improvement. That is compatible with an unresolved
detector preference. It must not be relabeled as evidence that the added detector
is worse.

